/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Thermodynamic equilibrium needs three kinds of balance at once.
   - For gas under a piston at rest, the forces on the piston balance.
   - p is the absolute gas pressure in Pa; A is the piston area in m²; pₐₜₘ is the atmospheric pressure in Pa; m is the piston mass and mₛ the load (sand) on it, in kg; g = 9.81 m/s².
   - A practical test of equilibrium is to read the gauges, wait and read again: an isolated system whose readings do not change is in equilibrium.
   - A steam turbine running at full load has steady readings: steam enters at, say, 500 °C and leaves at 60 °C, and each gauge holds still for hours.
   - Dump a heavy weight on a piston and the gas under it is squeezed fast: for a moment the gas next to the piston is at a higher pressure than the gas at the bottom, the piston overshoots and bounces, and eddies swirl.
   - Now add the same load one grain of sand at a time, waiting after each grain.
   - Because every intermediate state has one p and one V, the process is a line, and the work done at the moving boundary is W = ∫p dV, the area under that line (see displacement work).
   - A process is reversible if system and surroundings can both be returned to their first states with no trace left anywhere.
*/
