/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Zeroth law: when two bodies are each in thermal equilibrium with a third body, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other.
   - It sounds obvious, but it is an experimental fact, not a rule of logic: it is what lets a thermometer compare two bodies that never touch.
   - Equal temperatures do not mean equal energies.
   - A thermometer uses a thermometric property X, a quantity that changes steadily with how hot the body is: the length of a liquid column, the pressure of a gas held at constant volume, the resistance of a platinum wire, the emf of a thermocou
   - t is the temperature on this scale in °C; X is the property's reading; Xᵢ and Xₛ are its readings at the ice and steam points.
   - Different properties are not exactly linear in the same way, so two thermometers calibrated at the same fixed points agree at 0 and 100 °C but can disagree in between (second worked example).
   - A constant-volume gas thermometer comes closest.
   - T is in kelvin; p and pₜₚ are the gas pressures at the body and at the triple point of water, at the same volume.
   - The ideal-gas scale equals the thermodynamic (absolute) temperature scale, which the second law defines from the efficiency of reversible engines without any substance at all; that derivation sits with the thermodynamic temperature scale an
   - a temperature difference of 1 K equals 1 °C; 1 °C of difference equals 1.8 °F.
*/
