/* book Worked example: Q: (a) A thermometer reads 25.0 °C in body A and 25.0 °C in body B. A and B are then placed in contact. Which way does heat flow? Express the temperature in kelvin. (b) A constant-volume gas thermometer reads 60.00 kPa with its bulb at the triple point of water and 75.00 kPa in a water bath. Find the bath temperature in K and °C.
   1. (a) Thermometer C is in thermal equilibrium with A and, separately, with B, at the same reading.
   2. By the zeroth law, A and B are in thermal equilibrium with each other, so when they touch there is no net heat flow.
   3. T = t + 273.15 = 25.0 + 273.15 = 298.15 K (298.2 K to the precision of the reading).
   4. (b) T = 273.16 K × p/pₜₚ = 273.16 × 75.00 ÷ 60.00 = 273.16 × 1.2500 = 341.45 K.
   5. t = T − 273.15 = 341.45 − 273.15 = 68.30 °C.
   Answer: (a) No net heat flows; 298.15 K. (b) 341.45 K, which is 68.30 °C. */
